That role playing isn't _mandatory_ doesn't make it
any less valid as an _example of a reason_ someone
might have, to avoid a behavior.

That the Dev Team hasn't made it impossible in the
code to do something doesn't make doing that
something right for everyone.

I credit clone; other people are horrified at the
idea. Do I get to use the justification "but the Dev
Team lets me do it" as my sole metric of right or
wrong NetHack play to justify credit cloning?

Only if I want to provoke the Dev Team to remove it
as a game abuse.

Otherwise I'd better have some other reasons I
choose to do what I choose to do.

And _whatever_ those reasons are, they contradict a
claim like David Damerell's that there are
"absolutely no reasons" to do or not to do
something.

A claim of non-existence like that is refuted by
just _one_ example.

It doesn't have to be the world's most wonderful or
universally hailed example to work as a refutation.

It just has to exist at all.

